narrative:
A woman is fighting a losing battle with a fence post, swearing at it like it can be shamed into standing straight. She looks up when your shadow crosses her, sizes you up in about a second, and goes back to the post.

@Mira: You're not from around here. Good. Means you probably don't owe anybody money in this village yet.

She nods at the post. _It's not going to hold itself._

narrativeVariants.origin.civilian == true:
A woman is fighting a losing battle with a fence post, swearing at it like it can be shamed into standing straight. She looks up when your shadow crosses her - and something in your bearing makes her relax half a step.

@Mira: You've got farm hands. I can tell. Good - means you probably know a stubborn post when you see one.

She nods at it. _It's not going to hold itself._

narrativeVariants.origin.royal == true:
A woman is fighting a losing battle with a fence post, swearing at it like it can be shamed into standing straight. She catches sight of the signet at your hand and her whole posture changes - wary, not impressed.

@Mira: A lord's get, out here? Don't tell me House Aldric's come to collect taxes on a fence post.

She doesn't smile. _It's not going to hold itself, in case you were only here to watch._

narrative:
Between the two of you, the post goes in clean on the second heave, packed earth tamped tight around its base. Mira tests it with a shove that would have dropped it an hour ago. It doesn't move.

She doesn't thank you in words. She just stops fighting the fence and looks at you properly for the first time - like the post was never really the thing she needed help with, and you passing this small test means she can stop pretending it was.

@Mira: Huh. All right. You can stay long enough to hear the actual problem, then.

The post slips sideways on your second heave and lands harder than either of you meant, taking a chunk of the rail with it. Mira watches it fall with the flat expression of someone recalculating how much worse her afternoon just got.

@Mira: Right. Not a farm hand, then. Fine. Sit down before you break something that costs more than a fence.

She doesn't say it unkindly. She also doesn't offer to try again.

Mira wipes her hands on her trousers and finally stops pretending the fence is the problem.

@Mira: Fields have been bad since spring. Not the kind of bad you fix with better weather. And I've been - 

She stops herself.

@Mira: I've been taking grain from Old Ferrick's cousin's store. Small amounts. Nobody's noticed. I tell myself nobody's noticed.

She won't look at you now.

@Mira: I don't need a lecture. I need to know what you're going to do about it.

He shakes off the pull faster than you expected - some people just do, for reasons that have nothing to do with willpower and everything to do with luck - and what's left in his eyes when the moment passes isn't confusion. It's recognition. He knows exactly what you just tried to do to him.

@Hoarder: Get out of my sight before I tell every soul in this village what you are.

He doesn't open the stores. He barricades them further, and he remembers your face.

Mira finds you before you can find her. No fence post to fight this time - just her, and the particular stillness of someone who's already decided to ask for something and hates that it's come to this.

@Mira: I wouldn't ask if I had anyone else to ask. The farm doesn't make it through the season without help. Real help, not gold I'll owe back double.

She waits. _She's trying very hard not to look like she's waiting._
